      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: THE METHOD
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="method">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Method: Control Cases, Not Proof-Texts</h2>

        <p>Every Protestant who reads church history honestly eventually meets the
        argument: the practices Rome and the East hold dear — venerated icons,
        enshrined relics, prayers to the saints, an ever-expanding Marian devotion —
        are simply the tree that grew from the apostolic seed. To reject the tree,
        the argument runs, is to reject the seed. And the argument cannot be waved
        away with "it's not in the Bible," because some of the seed genuinely is in
        the Bible: bones that raise the dead, aprons that heal, a garment's hem that
        stops a hemorrhage.</p>

        <p>So a better method is needed than proof-texting in either direction.
        Scripture does not merely supply raw material for these practices — it
        contains <em>control cases</em>: places where the exact question later ages
        would face actually arose inside the canon and was adjudicated on the page.
        A holy object acquired a cult: what happened? A living apostle was offered
        veneration: what did he do? Someone sought contact with a departed saint:
        how did God respond? A heavenly figure delivered a message: how was it
        tested? These are not silences to be argued from. They are verdicts,
        recorded for us, in the very situations under dispute.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Rule of This Study</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Each practice gets its strongest defense
          before it gets a verdict. The Catholic and Orthodox cases below are not
          caricatures assembled to be knocked down; they are the arguments as John
          of Damascus, Basil, and Newman would want them stated. A conclusion that
          survives only against a weak opponent is not a conclusion. The claim of
          this study is that the verdicts survive the strong one — and that they are
          consistently generous toward the <em>seed</em> of each practice, and
          consistently severe toward the <em>cult</em>.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /method -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: CONCESSIONS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="concessions">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">What Must Be Conceded First</h2>

        <p>A case built on a position weaker than the truth will collapse in the
        first real conversation. Four concessions, then, made gladly and in full:</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Concession 1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">God works miracles through physical things.</div>
          <p>A dead man revives on contact with Elisha's bones
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Kings 13:20-21">2 Kings 13:20–21</a>).
          Elijah's mantle parts the Jordan
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Kings 2:13-14">2 Kings 2:13–14</a>). The woman
          with the flow of blood is healed touching the hem of Jesus' garment, and at
          Gennesaret "as many as touched it were made well"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 9:20-22">Matthew 9:20–22</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 14:36">14:36</a>). The sick are laid out
          hoping for Peter's shadow (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 5:15">Acts 5:15</a>);
          handkerchiefs that touched Paul's skin carry healing
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 19:11-12">Acts 19:11–12</a>). God heals with
          mud and spit, commands anointing with oil
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:14">James 5:14</a>), saves through water,
          feeds through bread and wine. Scripture is emphatically not embarrassed by
          matter.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Concession 2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The bodies of the saints are honored.</div>
          <p>Joseph's bones are carried out of Egypt for two generations in obedience
          to an oath and buried at Shechem
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Genesis 50:25">Genesis 50:25</a>;
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Joshua 24:32">Joshua 24:32</a>). Graves are marked,
          the dead are buried with care, and a body that housed the Holy Spirit
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 6:19">1 Corinthians 6:19</a>) awaits
          resurrection. Any Protestantism that shrugs at the body has forgotten its
          own doctrine.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Concession 3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Visions and heavenly messengers are real.</div>
          <p>Scripture is full of them, and
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:17">Acts 2:17</a> expects them to continue.
          "God cannot appear to people" is not a biblical premise, and no test below
          depends on it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Concession 4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Mary is blessed among women.</div>
          <p>"All generations will call me blessed"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 1:48">Luke 1:48</a>) is in the Bible, and
          low-church Protestants frequently under-deliver on it. Honoring Mary as the
          most privileged human participant in redemption is not the error under
          review. What is under review is precisely what she herself would review:
          the direction of devotion.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /concessions -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: TEST I — NEHUSHTAN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-1">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test I — The Nehushtan Test: When a Holy Object Gathers a Cult</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Numbers 21:8-9">Numbers 21:8–9</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Kings 18:3-5">2 Kings 18:3–5</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="John 3:14">John 3:14</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Judges 8:27">Judges 8:27</a> —
          applies to <strong>icons and relics</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>The Orthodox defense of icons is not sentimental; it is christological,
        and it was forged against a real heresy. John of Damascus: "I do not worship
        matter; I worship the Creator of matter who became matter for my sake… and
        who through matter worked my salvation." The invisible God has made Himself
        visible (<a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:15">Colossians 1:15</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:14">John 1:14</a>); to refuse all depiction
        of Christ, the iconodules argued, edges toward denying that the Incarnation
        really happened. Nor is God against images as such — He <em>commanded</em>
        them: cherubim of gold over the ark
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Exodus 25:18">Exodus 25:18</a>), cherubim woven
        through the tabernacle curtains, carved on the temple walls
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 6:29">1 Kings 6:29</a>), and the bronze
        serpent itself. The second commandment therefore forbids idols of false gods
        and attempts to image the divine <em>essence</em>, not sacred art in the
        place of worship. Basil supplies the mechanism: "the honor given to the image
        passes to the prototype" — a soldier kissing a photograph of his wife loves
        his wife, not the paper. And as for Hezekiah: what he destroyed was an object
        receiving <em>offerings</em> — incense, sacrificial worship, actual latria.
        That is abuse, and abuse does not abolish right use
        (<em>abusus non tollit usum</em>); we do not burn Bibles because some men
        idolize the book. For relics the logic is the same: God honored Elisha's
        bones with a miracle — dare we honor them less?</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>This is a serious argument, and half of it should simply be granted: God
        commands sacred art, and the second commandment is not a ban on images as
        such. The cherubim prove it. But notice what the cherubim also prove:
        God-commanded images existed at the very center of Israel's worship for a
        thousand years, and <em>no Israelite ever venerated one</em> — no incense to
        the cherubim, no kissing, no bowing toward them as objects of devotion.
        Scripture's own settlement is thus exactly the line the Reformed tradition
        drew: images <em>in</em> worship spaces, yes; devotion <em>to</em> them, no.
        The iconodule argument needs to carry devotion, not decoration, and the
        cherubim carry only decoration.</p>

        <p>The <em>abusus non tollit usum</em> reply to Hezekiah is the strongest
        move available, and it fails at a precise point: the text records no attempt
        at regulated use. Hezekiah does not purge the abuse and restore the serpent
        to honorable display; he pulverizes a God-commanded, miracle-working object
        and renames it "a piece of bronze" — and the narrator crowns him for it above
        all the kings of Judah (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Kings 18:5">2 Kings 18:5</a>).
        When Israel's history posed the exact question Nicaea II would face — what do
        you do when a holy object collects a cult? — the canonized answer was removal,
        not regulation. Nicaea II (787) did not merely choose regulation; it
        <em>anathematized</em> those who take Hezekiah's view, and its canon 7
        required relics for the consecration of every church. A development that ends
        by cursing the position Scripture praises has developed past its source.</p>

        <p>As for Basil's prototype principle — it explains how veneration
        <em>could</em> be innocent; it does not show that it is <em>authorized</em>,
        and "the honor passes to the prototype" was precisely what every
        sophisticated pagan said about his statue, as the church's own apologists
        knew when they refused the analogy. And the miracle texts themselves resist
        the inference "God honored it, so enshrine it." Elisha's bones revive a man
        by accident — a burial party fleeing raiders — and then: no shrine, no
        exhumation, no pilgrimage. Jesus redirects the healed woman from the garment
        to "your <em>faith</em>" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 9:22">Matthew 9:22</a>).
        Paul's aprons heal while Paul is alive and preaching, and Luke supplies the
        moral himself: "so the word of the Lord continued to increase"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 19:20">Acts 19:20</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">In every text the miracle is present and the
          cult is absent; the later practice added precisely everything the texts
          leave out. And in the one canonical case where a holy, miracle-working
          object did gather a cult, the response Scripture praises is not regulated
          veneration but removal.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-1 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: TEST II — THE REFUSED BOW
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-2">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test II — The Refused Bow: Veneration Offered to Creatures</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 10:25-26">Acts 10:25–26</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:11-15">Acts 14:11–15</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 19:10">Revelation 19:10</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 22:8-9">Revelation 22:8–9</a> —
          applies to <strong>veneration of saints &amp; icons; the dulia/latria
          distinction</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>The distinction between <em>latria</em> (adoration, owed to God alone)
        and <em>dulia</em> (honor, properly given to the excellent) is not an
        invention of decadence — it is in the Bible's own vocabulary of bowing.
        Obadiah falls on his face before Elijah
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Kings 18:7">1 Kings 18:7</a>); the sons of the
        prophets bow to the ground before Elisha
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Kings 2:15">2 Kings 2:15</a>); Israelites
        prostrate themselves before kings and prophets throughout the histories
        without a word of censure. Scripture plainly permits <em>proskynesis</em> as
        honor. The refused bows, on this reading, are special cases: at Lystra the
        crowd explicitly took Paul and Barnabas for <em>gods</em> ("the gods have
        come down to us!" — <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 14:11">Acts 14:11</a>), so
        of course they refused; Cornelius, a fresh convert from paganism, was
        plausibly confusing Peter with something more; and the angel's refusal in
        Revelation is angelic humility in the presence of the revelation of Jesus —
        or protection of John, overwhelmed mid-vision, from a bow sliding toward
        worship. None of these condemns the measured, liturgically disciplined honor
        the church gives its glorified heroes — honor which, as with icons,
        terminates on God who is "glorious in his saints."</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>Again, grant the true half: bowing as civil and social honor is
        biblically unproblematic. Nobody disputes honoring the excellent — the
        question is religious veneration of <em>glorified, departed</em> creatures
        in the context of worship, and it matters enormously that this is exactly
        the context of the two Revelation refusals. John is not a pagan confusing a
        man with Zeus; he is an apostle, inside a heavenly liturgy, bowing to a
        being he knows to be God's messenger — as pure a case of intended
        <em>dulia</em> toward a glorified creature as could be constructed. If the
        dulia/latria distinction were heaven's own framework, this is where heaven
        would apply it: "your veneration is welcome, John, so long as it is not
        adoration." Instead, twice, the categorical answer: "You must not do that…
        Worship God" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 19:10">Revelation 19:10</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 22:8-9">22:8–9</a>). The angel does not
        refine John's bow; he refuses it and redirects it — and "angelic humility"
        cannot explain a <em>command</em> ("you must not") grounded in a
        <em>reason</em> ("I am a fellow servant") that applies equally to every
        saint in glory.</p>

        <p>The pattern, then: in civil settings, bows pass without comment; in
        devotional settings, every creature offered veneration — apostle or angel,
        before pagans or inside heaven itself — refuses it on the spot, without
        exception. The practice under review does not ask whether we may honor the
        saints (we may, and should — naming churches for them, telling their
        stories, imitating their faith,
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 13:7">Hebrews 13:7</a>). It asks whether
        religious devotion may be <em>directed at</em> them in worship.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">On that question the canon's uniform
          testimony is the refused bow, and a system in which the glorified
          <em>accept</em> what apostles and angels uniformly declined owes an
          explanation the texts never supply.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-2 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: TEST III — THE ADDRESS OF PRAYER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-3">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test III — The Address of Prayer: Speaking to the Departed</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:14-16">Hebrews 4:14–16</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 8:19-20">Isaiah 8:19–20</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Samuel 28:7-19">1 Samuel 28</a> —
          applies to <strong>prayers to the saints and to Mary</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>Asking a saint's intercession, the defense runs, is not worship and not
        necromancy — it is the communion of saints taken seriously. Death cannot
        sever membership in the body of Christ
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 8:38-39">Romans 8:38–39</a>: neither death
        nor life can separate us); the departed are alive to God, indeed more alive
        than we (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 20:38">Luke 20:38</a>); and Scripture
        shows them aware of and engaged with earth: the martyrs under the altar cry
        out about events in history
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 6:9-10">Revelation 6:9–10</a>), the
        elders in heaven present "the prayers of the saints" before the throne
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 5:8">Revelation 5:8</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 8:3-4">8:3–4</a>), and a "great cloud of
        witnesses" surrounds the runners below
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:1">Hebrews 12:1</a>). In the Lord's own
        parable, a man in the afterlife converses with Abraham and asks him to
        intervene for his living brothers
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 16:24-31">Luke 16:24–31</a>) — dialogue
        across death is not unthinkable to Jesus. And
        <a class="ref" data-ref="James 5:16">James 5:16</a> says the prayer of a
        <em>righteous</em> person has great power: who is more righteous than the
        perfected just (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:23">Hebrews 12:23</a>)?
        What is condemned at Endor is the séance — conjuring the dead for hidden
        knowledge — not a family member in Christ saying "pray for me" across the
        veil. If it is right to ask a friend's prayers, it cannot become sin merely
        because the friend now stands closer to God.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>This is the most sympathetic of all the defenses, and its premises are
        largely true: the departed are alive, they are aware in glory, heaven is
        interested in earth, and asking intercession from the living is commanded.
        What the argument cannot produce is the one thing it needs: a single
        instance, command, or encouragement of an earthly believer
        <em>addressing</em> a departed saint. Scripture contains hundreds of
        recorded prayers — patriarchs, psalmists, prophets, apostles, the Lord
        Himself — and every one is addressed to God. The heavenly scenes cut the
        other way on inspection: in
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 5:8">Revelation 5:8</a> and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 8:3-4">8:3–4</a> the prayers of the
        saints ascend <em>to the throne</em>, and heaven handles them there — the
        passage describes prayers to God being presented, not prayers to the
        presenters. The martyrs of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 6:10">Revelation 6:10</a> cry "O
        Sovereign Lord" — the departed themselves pray <em>to God</em>. The "cloud
        of witnesses" of <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 12:1">Hebrews 12:1</a> are
        witnesses in the sense the whole of chapter 11 establishes: witnesses
        <em>to faith</em>, whose lives testify to us — and the very next verse
        directs the runner's gaze not to the cloud but to Jesus. And Luke 16 is a
        parable whose traffic runs opposite to the practice: no one on earth
        addresses the dead, and the one request for cross-boundary communication —
        send Lazarus to my brothers — is <em>refused</em>, with the reason given:
        "they have Moses and the Prophets; let them hear them"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 16:29">Luke 16:29</a>). The Lord's one
        afterlife story ends by pointing the living back to Scripture rather than
        opening a channel to the departed.</p>

        <p>The Endor distinction — invocation is not necromancy — is fair as far as
        it goes: the séance sought hidden knowledge, and the church's prayers do
        not. But the distinction only shows the practice is not the <em>worst</em>
        thing; it does not show it is a <em>warranted</em> thing, and warrant is the
        issue, because the alternative on offer is not silence but the throne.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          For there is one God, and there is one mediator between God and men, the
          man Christ Jesus.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Because that mediator is a sympathetic high priest, "let us then with
        confidence draw near to the throne of grace"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 4:16">Hebrews 4:16</a>). The whole
        pastoral energy of Hebrews is that ordinary believers now hold the access
        once reserved for the high priest alone.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A piety that routes requests through the
          glorified quietly declines the privilege the gospel purchased — and
          Isaiah's principle stands over the whole terrain: when urged to seek the
          dead on behalf of the living, "to the teaching and to the testimony!"
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 8:19-20">Isaiah 8:19–20</a>).</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-3 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: TEST IV — MARY IN CHARACTER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-4">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test IV — Mary in Character: The Direction of Her Words</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 1:46-48">Luke 1:46–48</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="John 2:5">John 2:5</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 11:27-28">Luke 11:27–28</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:14">Acts 1:14</a> —
          applies to <strong>Marian dogmas, Marian titles, apparition messages</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>The Marian doctrines, their defenders insist, are not devotional excess
        but exegesis — typological exegesis of the kind the New Testament itself
        practices. Luke writes the Visitation over the template of the ark's journey
        in <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Samuel 6:9-11">2 Samuel 6</a>: Elizabeth's
        "why is this granted to me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 1:43">Luke 1:43</a>) echoes David's "how can
        the ark of the LORD come to me?"; John leaps as David danced; Mary remains
        three months as the ark remained with Obed-edom. If Mary is the ark of the
        new covenant — the vessel of the Word, the manna, the true High Priest —
        then her purity and her preservation follow as fittingness: the ark was made
        of incorruptible acacia and overlaid with gold. The New Eve theme is
        genuinely early and genuinely weighty: Irenaeus, a disciple of Polycarp who
        knew John, writes that "the knot of Eve's disobedience was loosed by the
        obedience of Mary" (c. AD 180). Gabriel greets her with a title,
        <em>kecharitomene</em>, "full of grace"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 1:28">Luke 1:28</a>). The woman of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 12:1">Revelation 12</a> is crowned in
        heaven. And each Marian definition, Rome argues, has historically served
        Christology: <em>Theotokos</em> (431) protected the unity of Christ's person
        against Nestorius; the dogmas honor the Son by honoring what grace
        accomplished in the mother. Even her intercession is Cana continued: she
        notices the need, brings it to Jesus, and tells the servants — as she tells
        us — "do whatever he tells you."</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>Take the strongest pieces first. <em>Theotokos</em> is right, and
        Protestants confess it: the one Mary bore is God, and Ephesus 431 was
        defending Christ, not exalting Mary. Irenaeus's New Eve is real and early —
        but read what it actually asserts: a parallel of <em>obedience</em>, Mary's
        faithful yes against Eve's faithless no. It says nothing of sinless
        conception or bodily assumption, and the same Irenaeus reads Cana as Jesus
        <em>correcting</em> his mother's timing ("Woman, what does this have to do
        with me?" — <a class="ref" data-ref="John 2:4">John 2:4</a>). The ark
        typology is genuinely beautiful — and typology is illustration, not
        demonstration. The New Testament uses types to display what is independently
        revealed (Jonah displays the resurrection; it could never have
        <em>established</em> it). A chain that runs ark → acacia wood →
        incorruptibility → therefore Mary could not decay is not exegesis producing
        dogma; it is dogma shopping for imagery — which is why the church waited
        until 1950 to bind it, and why Pius XII's definition cites fittingness
        rather than evidence. <em>Kecharitomene</em> means "favored one," and Paul
        uses the same verb of every believer
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 1:6">Ephesians 1:6</a>); the woman of
        Revelation 12 bears Israel's twelve stars, flees into the wilderness, and is
        pursued — the mother of the Messiah as the covenant people, which even many
        Catholic exegetes grant as the primary sense.</p>

        <p>Then run the character test, which needs no verdict on typology at all.
        The biblical Mary speaks little, and everything she says points away from
        herself: "my spirit rejoices in God my <em>Savior</em>"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 1:47">Luke 1:47</a>) — she confesses her own
        need of saving; her last recorded words are seven words of deflection, "Do
        whatever <em>he</em> tells you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 2:5">John 2:5</a>);
        when a woman blesses her womb, Jesus redirects the devotion to "those who
        hear the word of God and keep it"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 11:27-28">Luke 11:27–28</a>); her final
        appearance shows her <em>praying with</em> the church
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 1:14">Acts 1:14</a>), never receiving prayer
        from it. The later structure runs the other direction: dogmas her medieval
        champions' own greatest doctors opposed (Bernard of Clairvaux and Thomas
        Aquinas both rejected the Immaculate Conception it took until 1854 to
        define), an Assumption whose earliest witnesses are fifth-century apocrypha
        the Roman Gelasian Decree itself listed among rejected books, titles —
        Mediatrix of All Graces, Co-Redemptrix — that press toward the very
        mediation <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a>
        reserves, and apparitions whose recurring message is devotion to <em>her
        own</em> Immaculate Heart.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The Cana defense concedes the standard the
          practice must meet: a Mary who brings needs to Jesus and points servants
          to Him. Measured by that standard — her standard — a heavenly figure whose
          consistent theme is the expansion of her own role is speaking out of
          character. The Magnificat is the control; the system fails it.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-4 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: TEST V — THE ANGEL FROM HEAVEN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-5">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test V — The Angel from Heaven: Phenomena versus Content</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8">Galatians 1:8</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 13:1-3">Deuteronomy 13:1–3</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 11:14">2 Corinthians 11:14</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:1">1 John 4:1</a> —
          applies to <strong>apparitions; miracles at shrines and relics</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>God has always confirmed His messengers with signs — Moses before
        Pharaoh, Elijah on Carmel, the Lord Himself: "even though you do not believe
        me, believe the works" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 10:38">John 10:38</a>);
        Jesus was "attested to you by God with mighty works and wonders and signs"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Acts 2:22">Acts 2:22</a>). If God still
        authenticates, would we not expect wonders precisely where His church is?
        And the church is no credulous judge: the overwhelming majority of claimed
        apparitions are rejected or shelved; Lourdes maintains a medical bureau
        staffed with physicians, including skeptics; canonization miracles face
        formal scrutiny. The approved cases are the survivors of institutional
        skepticism, not its absence. Judge by fruits, as the Lord commanded
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:16">Matthew 7:16</a>): millions of
        conversions, confessions, and quiet holiness at Lourdes and Fatima;
        Guadalupe evangelizing a continent. And Rome herself files all of this under
        <em>private</em> revelation — no Catholic is obliged to believe any of it —
        so the apparitions are offered not as new doctrine but as heaven's pastoral
        encouragement, pointing (say their defenders) always to prayer, penance,
        and the Eucharist.</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>Here Scripture's control is the most explicit of all, because the Bible
        legislates for exactly this scenario — a luminous heavenly visitor bearing
        religious content:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But even if we or an angel from heaven should preach to you a gospel
          contrary to the one we preached to you, let him be accursed.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8">Galatians 1:8</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Paul does not say "examine whether the angel is genuine." He rules that
        the <em>message</em> is tested against the deposit, and the vision's
        impressiveness counts for nothing.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Deuteronomy 13:1-3">Deuteronomy 13:1–3</a> is more
        stunning still: even if the sign <em>comes to pass</em>, a message that
        redirects devotion disqualifies the messenger — "for the LORD your God is
        testing you." This answers the appeal to biblical signs at its root: in
        Scripture, signs authenticate messengers whose message is already consonant
        with prior revelation — the canon itself splits sign from warrant the moment
        content redirects devotion. So the fruits test is right, but content is the
        first fruit: and the messages' recurring content — consecration to the
        apparition's own Immaculate Heart, scapular promises, reparation to Mary —
        is precisely what Test IV found out of character and
        <a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 2:5">1 Timothy 2:5</a> fire-walls. That
        verdict stands <em>regardless of the mechanics</em>, which is what makes
        this test so economical: no Protestant needs to prove fraud at Fatima,
        though the mechanics rarely survive inspection either — the century of
        documentary silence after 1531 at Guadalupe, with the missionary Sahagún
        warning in the 1570s that the Tepeyac cult continued the goddess Tonantzin;
        Lourdes's own bureau recognizing about seventy cures from seven thousand
        claims across 160 years, a ratio hard to distinguish from spontaneous
        remission; a Fatima crowd whose members saw different suns while no
        observatory saw anything.</p>

        <p>Two of the defense's own points, pressed firmly, become the reply. The
        church's institutional skepticism proves that even Rome believes apparitions
        are guilty until proven innocent — the Protestant merely runs the same
        tribunal with Galatians 1:8 seated on the bench. And the "private
        revelation" classification concedes the decisive point: if the deposit of
        faith stands complete without Fatima (Catechism §§66–67), then Fatima cannot
        be evidence <em>for</em> the system — an argument that leans on apparitions
        to enter the Catholic Church asks them to bear more weight than the Catholic
        Church assigns them.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The wonders prove too much in any case: the
          mass sightings at Zeitoun (1968) hovered over a <em>Coptic Orthodox</em>
          church; if phenomena validated a communion, they would validate every
          communion at once — which is to say, none. Doctrine tests miracles;
          miracles do not establish doctrine.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-5 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: TEST VI — THE CLOSED DEPOSIT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="test-6">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Test VI — The Closed Deposit: New Dogma Made Binding</h2>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Control Texts &amp; Scope</span>
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 1:3">Jude 3</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 1:1-2">Hebrews 1:1–2</a> ·
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 1:8-9">Galatians 1:8–9</a> —
          applies to <strong>the 1854 &amp; 1950 Marian dogmas; papal infallibility
          (1870)</strong>.
        </div>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">The Strongest Case for the Practice</h3>

        <p>Development, Rome answers, is not addition. The church creates no
        revelation; she comes to explicit consciousness of what was always
        implicitly hers — and the Protestant himself lives off exactly such
        developments. <em>Homoousios</em> is not in the Bible; the word was defined
        at Nicaea in 325 after arian Christians objected to it on
        sola-scriptura-sounding grounds. The doctrine of the Trinity, the two
        natures of Chalcedon, the canon of Scripture itself — none arrives fully
        stated in the apostolic age; each was drawn out over centuries by the
        church, to whom Christ promised, "the Spirit of truth… will guide you into
        all truth" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 16:13">John 16:13</a>), and whom
        Paul calls "the pillar and buttress of the truth"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Timothy 3:15">1 Timothy 3:15</a>). Nor was
        everything committed to writing: "stand firm and hold to the traditions that
        you were taught by us, either by our spoken word or by our letter"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a>);
        "were every one of them to be written, I suppose the world itself could not
        contain the books" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 21:25">John 21:25</a>).
        If you accept the development that gave you Nicaea and your New Testament,
        on what principle do you refuse the developments of 1854 and 1950 — except
        that you privately dislike them?</p>

        <h3 class="tg-subsection">Under the Control Test</h3>

        <p>This is the strongest argument in the entire system, and it deserves the
        most careful answer. The Nicene comparison is the load-bearing wall — and it
        fails on the direction of the evidence. <em>Homoousios</em> is a new
        <em>word</em> for old <em>content</em>: the deity of Christ is asserted
        across the New Testament
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 1:1">John 1:1</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 20:28">20:28</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Philippians 2:6">Philippians 2:6</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Colossians 1:15-20">Colossians 1:15–20</a>), Christ
        was worshipped from the beginning, Pliny's pagan report (c. AD 112) already
        describes Christians singing "to Christ as to a god," and the ante-Nicene
        fathers confess His divinity for two centuries before the council. Nicaea
        summarized what every church already possessed in its own documents. Now run
        the same trace on the Assumption: no text, no early father, no liturgy, no
        burial tradition — nothing until fifth-century apocrypha that Rome's own
        decree rejected, and then a definition in 1950. The Immaculate Conception
        was not merely unattested but <em>opposed</em> — by Bernard, by Aquinas —
        inside the tradition itself. Development-as-explication must be able to show
        the implicit content early, in the deposit; Newman's own historical labor
        could not, which is why his <em>Essay</em> (1845) replaces evidence of
        presence with marks of legitimate growth. The canon comparison fails the
        same way: the church <em>recognized</em> apostolic books already functioning
        as Scripture — a receiving act, not a producing one — and on Rome's account
        the infallible canon list waited until Trent (1546), which proves God's
        people can live for centuries by recognition without an infallible
        definer.</p>

        <p>The remaining texts prove less than they must.
        <a class="ref" data-ref="John 16:13">John 16:13</a> was spoken to the
        apostles, whose Spirit-guided teaching we possess — as apostolic Scripture;
        stretched to underwrite whatever the church later defines, the promise
        becomes unfalsifiable, a blank check no forgery could ever bounce. The
        "traditions" of
        <a class="ref" data-ref="2 Thessalonians 2:15">2 Thessalonians 2:15</a> are
        the gospel Paul had <em>already delivered</em> to that congregation, spoken
        and written — not a parallel secret stream surfacing after eighteen
        centuries; and it is precisely the appeal to unwritten tradition that can
        never be checked which lets any novelty claim apostolic pedigree. Jude's
        standard cuts the knot: contend "for the faith that was <em>once for all
        delivered</em> to the saints" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Jude 1:3">Jude 3</a>).
        The God who spoke in many ways has now spoken by His Son
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 1:1-2">Hebrews 1:1–2</a>).</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">Verdict</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">Nicaea guarded the deposit against
          subtraction; 1854, 1870, and 1950 <em>bound consciences</em> — under
          anathema — to content the deposit cannot be shown to contain and the
          tradition's own doctors denied. Whatever the early church was, it was not
          a communion where denying the bodily Assumption severed you from the
          faith, for the sufficient reason that no one in it had heard of the bodily
          Assumption. A church that can reopen the deposit has ceased to guard it —
          and Galatians 1:8 stands at that door.</p>
        </div>

      </section><!-- /test-6 -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: THE HISTORICAL LEDGER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="ledger">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Historical Ledger</h2>

        <p>The control tests give the biblical verdict; honest history supplies the
        timeline. Each practice below has a real seed — usually legitimate — and a
        traceable arc from seed to mandate. The dates matter, because "the church
        has always done this" is a claim about dates.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Practice</th>
              <th>Apostolic era</th>
              <th>2nd–3rd century</th>
              <th>4th century onward</th>
              <th>Made binding</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Icons</strong></td>
              <td>No devotional images; the refused-bow pattern</td>
              <td>Catacomb art — didactic, not venerated; Elvira (c. 305) forbids church images</td>
              <td>Veneration grows 6th–7th c.; contested by Epiphanius, then the iconoclast crisis</td>
              <td><strong>Nicaea II, 787</strong> — refusal anathematized</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Relics</strong></td>
              <td>Burial with honor; graves stay closed</td>
              <td>Polycarp's bones buried, grave visited (c. 156) — honor, no cult</td>
              <td>Translations, division, enshrinement, healing shrines; Vigilantius objects (c. 400), Jerome answers</td>
              <td><strong>Nicaea II, canon 7</strong> — no altar without relics</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Prayer to saints</strong></td>
              <td>All recorded prayer addressed to God</td>
              <td>Memorial meals at martyrs' tombs; first invocations appear</td>
              <td>Invocation normalized alongside the martyr cult</td>
              <td>Assumed by liturgy; reaffirmed at <strong>Trent, 1563</strong></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Marian dogmas</strong></td>
              <td>"Blessed among women"; God her Savior</td>
              <td>New Eve parallel in Irenaeus (c. 180); perpetual-virginity tradition spreading</td>
              <td><em>Theotokos</em> (431, a christological ruling); Assumption legends from 5th-c. apocrypha; Immaculate Conception opposed by Bernard &amp; Aquinas</td>
              <td><strong>1854 &amp; 1950</strong> — de fide</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Apparitions</strong></td>
              <td>None recorded</td>
              <td>None; earliest legend (c. 250s) reported a century later</td>
              <td>Medieval shrine foundations; the flood is modern: 1830, 1846, 1858, 1879, 1917…</td>
              <td><strong>Never binding</strong> — by Rome's own rule</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Reading the Table</span>
          Every row runs left to right from a seed the Bible knows to a mandate it
          doesn't — and the binding column clusters late.
        </div>

      </section><!-- /ledger -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: DEVELOPMENT OR CORRUPTION?
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="development">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Development or Corruption?</h2>

        <p>Is all this "pure development," invented from nothing? No — that
        overstates the case, and the overstatement is exploitable. Honoring the
        martyred dead is legitimate and ancient; God's use of matter is biblical;
        Mary is blessed; the New Eve is second-century. The practices grew from real
        seeds through identifiable stages into forms the biblical texts do not
        contain.</p>

        <p>But that is exactly why the question cannot be settled by the mere fact
        of development — and here Newman's theory concedes more than it defends. The
        theory of development (1845) exists <em>because</em> straightforward
        historical continuity fails for these practices; it is the sophisticated
        admission that the fourth century is not the first, and the nineteenth is
        not the fourth. Once development is admitted, everything turns on a
        criterion: which developments are the acorn becoming the oak, and which are
        the mistletoe colonizing it? A criterion must stand outside the thing it
        measures. The magisterium cannot be the criterion of its own developments —
        that is the fox auditing the henhouse. The only criterion that stands
        outside every century equally is the apostolic deposit, which is simply the
        Protestant principle stated as a method.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">The Practices Were Contested as They Emerged</span>
          The church of the developing centuries was not unanimous. The Council of
          Elvira forbade images in churches while the catacombs were still being
          painted. Epiphanius of Salamis tore down an image-bearing curtain and
          called it contrary to Scripture. Vigilantius objected to the relic vigils
          in terms any Reformer would recognize, and Jerome's furious rebuttal is
          the proof the dispute was live. Augustine frankly lamented the
          "sepulchre-worshippers" among the laity. The Reformation did not import a
          foreign objection into church history; it took the side of an argument
          that had been running inside the church all along — and it took the side
          that Nehushtan, the refused bows, and the address of prayer had already
          taken inside the canon.
        </div>

      </section><!-- /development -->

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: THE NARROW PATH
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="path">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Narrow Path: Polycarp's Church</h2>

        <p>Around the year 156, the church at Smyrna watched their bishop Polycarp —
        a disciple of the apostle John — burned alive for refusing to say "Caesar is
        Lord." Afterward they gathered his bones, "more precious than jewels and
        finer than gold," buried them, and resolved to meet at the grave each year
        to remember his martyrdom. And when critics insinuated that the Christians
        would now worship Polycarp, the church answered in a sentence that is the
        whole of this study in miniature:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Him we worship as the Son of God; but the martyrs we love as disciples and
          imitators of the Lord — as they deserve — on account of their unsurpassable
          devotion to their own King and Teacher.
          <cite>Martyrdom of Polycarp 17 (c. AD 156)</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>There is the line, drawn by the generation that knew the apostles:
        <em>love without invocation, honor without cult, memory without
        mediation</em>. Bury the dead with reverence. Name the martyrs and tell
        their stories. Tend the graves, keep the anniversaries, thank God for Mary
        and mean it. Worship Christ alone, pray to God alone through the one
        mediator, and when any object, image, bone, or vision begins to collect the
        devotion that belongs to Him — remember which king Scripture calls
        incomparable, and what he did with the bronze serpent.</p>

        <p>This is not a grudging middle position. It is the earliest position on
        record, it passes every control test the canon supplies, and it answers the
        real hunger behind the ancient practices — the conviction that bodies
        matter, that the dead in Christ are alive, that the church spans heaven and
        earth — without surrendering the privilege the gospel purchased: confident,
        direct access to the throne of grace. Those who venerate icons and invoke
        the saints are not, for that, outside of Christ; the essentials of the faith
        are older and deeper than this dispute, and the arguments above were made by
        serious believers seeking to honor the same Lord. But for the believer
        troubled by the claim that history demands these practices, the finding is
        the opposite: tested by the Word at full strength, and dated by the record,
        the narrow path was marked out long ago — and it runs through Smyrna, not
        around it.</p>

      </section><!-- /path -->

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Martyrdom of Polycarp</cite>, c. AD 156. Public domain.
          <em>The earliest window onto how the generation after the apostles honored
          its martyred dead — chapters 17–18 are the primary source for the "honor
          without cult" position, and cut both ways in instructive fashion.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Three Treatises on the Divine Images</cite>, John of Damascus.
          SVS Press, 2003 [orig. c. 730]. <em>The strongest patristic defense of
          icon veneration — the incarnational argument at its source, essential for
          representing the Orthodox case fairly.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>What It Means to Be Protestant</cite>, Gavin Ortlund. Zondervan,
          2024. <em>The current standard for the historical-Protestant case —
          patient, primary-source-driven, and charitable to Rome and the East;
          directly treats icons, relics, saints, and the Marian dogmas.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Shape of Sola Scriptura</cite>, Keith Mathison. Canon Press,
          2001. <em>Distinguishes sola scriptura from nuda scriptura; grounds the
          "closed deposit" test in the classical Protestant doctrine of
          authority.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>An Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine</cite>, John Henry
          Newman. University of Notre Dame Press, 1989 [orig. 1845]. <em>The most
          influential Catholic account of development — read it to test the
          "development or corruption" section against its strongest source.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>A Treatise on Relics</cite>, John Calvin. 1543. Public domain.
          <em>The Reformation's most direct engagement with the relic cult —
          polemical but grounded in an inventory of the actual claims.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Miracles: The Credibility of the New Testament Accounts</cite>,
          Craig Keener. Baker Academic, 2011. <em>Documents credible healing
          reports across Catholic, Orthodox, and Protestant contexts alike — the
          evidence that wonders cannot arbitrate ecclesiology.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Orthodox Church</cite>, Timothy Ware (Kallistos). Penguin,
          1963 / 1993. <em>The standard sympathetic introduction to Orthodox
          theology of icons, saints, and Tradition — the other side, stated by
          itself.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

